Character Model and Related Data

10.14.25 v1.0
The Character model is envisioned to be the most used model throughout the application. Serving as a connection Model to a majority of the data simply because all other data is based around each unique character. Now this may or may not mean (as I am still learning and figuring this out) that the character class must contain all other data types within it. There are a variety of ways to connect data, and I am still discovering those and choosing which might best fit what I am trying to do. For now, I will focus on the Core Character data. 
*Note: Some of this data will be abstracted into other subclasses depending on what best fits the needs of the application

Character Model Data:
- Player Name : str
- Character Name : str
- Character Class List : List<CharacterClass>**
- Character Level : Derived from Character Class List
- Ability Scores
- Race
- Proficiency Bonus

Some additional Notes
* Class is a large enough data model that it can stand on its own and probably should considering how much data is determined based on the class and or class list
* Ability Scores might be enough to have its own model due to the multiple uses it has and its specificity of naming convention. An enum for its name and a specific Ability class might be useful

Class Model Data
- Class Name : str, enum?
- Class Level : int
- SubClass Name : str, enum?
- Spell Casting Ability : Ability
- Spell Save DC : int - Derived from Spell Casting Ability
- Spell Attack Bonus : int - Derived from Spell Casting Ability and Proficiency
- Spell List : List<Spell>** Does it need to be in this class? or can it be separated?
- Total Spell Slots : Dictionary<SpellLevel, int> - Derived from Level
- Available Spell Slots : Dictionary<SpellLevel, int> - Derived from user input and Total Spell Slots
Ability Model Data
- Ability Name : str enum?
- Ability Score : int
- Ability Modifier : int - Derived from Ability Score

